We have
\begin{align*} \mathbf{R} &= \frac{1}{\|\mathbf{v}\|^2} \begin{pmatrix} v_1^2 - v_2^2 & 2v_1 v_2 \\ 2v_1 v_2 & v_2^2 - v_1^2 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{3^2 + 1^2} \begin{pmatrix} 3^2 - 1^2 & 2(3)(1) \\ 2(3)(1) & 1^2 - 3^2 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{10} \begin{pmatrix} 9 - 1 & 6 \\ 6 & 1 - 9 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \frac{1}{10} \begin{pmatrix} 8 & 6 \\ 6 & -8 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 8/10 & 6/10 \\ 6/10 & -8/10 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 4/5 & 3/5 \\ 3/5 & -4/5 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}Then
\begin{align*} \mathbf{R}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 4/5 & 3/5 \\ 3/5 & -4/5 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 4/5 & 3/5 \\ 3/5 & -4/5 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} (4/5)(4/5) + (3/5)(3/5) & (4/5)(3/5) + (3/5)(-4/5) \\ (3/5)(4/5) + (-4/5)(3/5) & (3/5)(3/5) + (-4/5)(-4/5) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 16/25 + 9/25 & 12/25 - 12/25 \\ 12/25 - 12/25 & 9/25 + 16/25 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 25/25 & 0 \\ 0 & 25/25 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \mathbf{I}. \end{align*}

\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}

original_answer_raw:
Construct a right triangle with legs 1 and $x.$  Let the angle opposite the side length $x$ be $\theta.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C;

A = (2,1.8);
B = (0,0);
C = (2,0);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(A,C,B,8));

label("$\theta$", B + (0.7,0.3));
label("$1$", (B + C)/2, S);
label("$x$", (A + C)/2, E);
label("$\sqrt{x^2 + 1}$", (A + B)/2, NW);
[/asy]

Then $\tan \theta = x,$ so $\theta = \arctan x.$  Then
\[\cos (\arctan x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}},\]so
\[\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}} = x.\]Squaring both sides, we get
\[\frac{1}{x^2 + 1} = x^2,\]so $x^4 + x^2 - 1 = 0.$  By the quadratic formula,
\[x^2 = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}.\]Since $x^2$ is positive,
\[x^2 = \boxed{\frac{-1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}}.\]

