We can expand the determinant as follows:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 0 & b - a & c - a \\ a - b & 0 & c - b \\ a - c & b - c & 0 \end{vmatrix} &= -(b - a) \begin{vmatrix} a - b & c - b \\ a - c & 0 \end{vmatrix} + (c - a) \begin{vmatrix} a - b & 0 \\ a - c & b - c \end{vmatrix} \\
&= -(b - a)(-(c - b)(a - c)) + (c - a)(a - b)(b - c) \\
&= \boxed{0}.
\end{align*}

When using the distributive property for the first time, we add the product of $2p^2 - 1$ and $3p^2$ to the product of $2p^2 - 1$ and $4$:

\begin{align*}
(2p^2 - 1)(3p^2 + 4) &= (2p^2 - 1) \cdot (3p^2) + (2p^2 - 1) \cdot 4
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again and combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
(3p^2)(2p^2 - 1) + 4(2p^2 - 1) &= 6p^4 - 3p^2 + 8p^2 - 4\\
&= \boxed{6p^4 + 5p^2 - 4}
\end{align*}

