\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} 9x - 4y &= 6 \\ 7x + y &= 17\end{align*}
We can solve this system using substitution or elimination. Let's use elimination. Multiply the second equation by 4 to eliminate $y$:

smollm2_with_partial_cot_raw:
\begin{align*} \label{eq:2} 9x - 4y &= 6 \\ 28x + 4y &= 70 \qquad (\text{Multiply by } 4) \\ \end{align*}
Add equations ([eq:1]) and ([eq:2]):
\begin{align*} (9x - 4y) + (28x + 4y) &= 6 + 70 \\ 37x &= 76 \\ x &= \frac{76}{37} \\ \end{align*}
Substitute $x = \frac{76}{37}$ into equation ([eq:1]):
\begin{align*} 9(\frac{76}{37}) - 4y &= 6 \\ \frac{684}{37} - 4y &= 6 \\ -4y &= 6 - \frac{684}{37} \\ y &= \frac{-251 + 684}{37} \\ y &= \frac{433}{37} \\ \end{align*}
Thus, the intersection point is $\boxed{\left( \frac{76}{37}, \frac{433}{37} \right)}$.

